Alright! My second ascension. I'm glad I decided to go through when I
felt like starting a new game (see Late Game Motivation thread). The
planes were fun, and Gehenom was interesting.

First break was a big scroll shop on level 2 or three, lots of identify
scrolls. A lot of scrolls were identified there, one was a blessed
genocide. Bebye all mimics! The early game was helped by enchanting the
shirt to +5 and the lucky finding of a +5 dwarvish mithril mail.
Minetown temple was co-aligned, Spoon offered till he got a good weapon
(vorpal blade).

On level 11 the game changed completely. I stumbled upon the bones of
one of my priest who ate medusa meat. He died at his stash level. It
was hard to reach the stash, as the former pets were very very tough
(Jabberwock, Olog Hai, Titan and Gray Dragon.) It took all of Spoon's
cunning and ressources. At least the Jabberwock was no match for the
blade.

But the stash! All spellbooks, many many wands - including one wishing
(1:0). Magicbane, Grayswandir, the mitre of Holiness - and countless
other goodies. That priest used to wear a GDSM and an amulet of
reflection. Spoon decided he would rather keep his neck free for life
saving - which turned out to be a wise decision, so he wished for a
SDSM. Spoon read all blessed spellbook and was glad to find identify
amongst those. It helped sort through the stash.

By the way, a lawful pet of mine eventually picked up the Mitre of
Holiness and wore it for a long while. Isn't it a bit weird? Titan sure
aren't priests!

Skipped sokoban, did the quest as soon as level 14 was reached - with
the help of a titan pet. Ludios was done with the help of three purple
worm pets (Buuurp!)

I busted many rings of increase damage with the PYEC. I ended up with
two +6 rings. Makes for very very deadly fighting.

After the castle (Buuurp!) I wished for a ring of TC and ate a wood
nymph. Spoon really hated losing a slot of increase damage, so he
decided to curse genocide tengus. It took 4 scrolls before he felt in
control!

Gehenom was not all looted, a path was dig (by hand) in between all
stairs. Only the minimum set of demon lords were met. The vibrating
square was found at level 52. I ditched my pets around on Orcus level -
it was getting really tedious to gather them up around the stairs.
Around that time I had forgotten my spells too. I decided not to go
back to my stash (on level 11) and Spoon wished for a spellbook of
identify. Lazy lazy.

Before doing his final preparation, Spoon cleared all levels but the
last of the wizard tower and looted wimpy Vlad's tower. He died on the
zoo in the wizard's tower, when he fell in a pit wielding a cockatrice
corpse - I'm glad he had his amulet of LS. After that Spoon went back
to minetown to buy some candles, polypiled a bit his potions and
scrolls and did some alchemy for HPs. On the way he also played a lot
with magic traps, trying to get some Pw out of them. He got a few of
those, but he lack the patience to milk them all.

On the way back to the the wizard. A mind flayer got lucky and was able
to suck some of Spoon's grey matter. It made the navigation of gehenom
a bit more painful, as many levels got forgotten. The items were less
of a problem with the ID spell always available.

The Wizard got wasted in hand to hand. +6 Grayswandir, +7 silver saber
and +12 increase damage makes for a quick fight. Moloch's Sanctum was a
painful process, as many ants were summoned. A generous cockatrice left
a corpse which simplified  the process a lot.

Just to get a feel of the length of the dungeon, I decided to NOT use
any teleportation mean on the way up. It is not so bad. Except on some
levels that were forgotten and where I ended up searching for the
stairs everywhere. (The travel command helps a lot when you have no
pets - it's not much used if you have a couple of pets.) The mysterous
force was rather mild. And Rodney always fell quickly to Spoon's
blades. He double troubled only once.

The elemental planes were rather easy, but interesting. An archon on
the plane of fire was long to dispatch. The plane of water was rather
long too. Air was straightforward. Rodney only showed up on earth once.

Astral was a bit rougher. I first went toward pestilence. Player
monsters were more trouble than pestilence, which fell to a single
blow. As I was busy dealing with a bunch of priests and angels and
player monsters, I didn't notice I was hungry - then weak... then
fainting. They ganged up on him when he fell to the ground and were
able to go through his many HPs. But wait... his amulet saved him
again. The altar was Blind Io's... So back and toward Death Spoon went.
Wimpy death. Easy to kill too. The altar was The Lady's. End of story.
